## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates candidates' ability to design scalable, multi-tenant operating system components that handle real-world production workloads. It tests understanding of kernel-level I/O subsystems, resource isolation, and fairness algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- Per-container quotas via cgroups blkio controller
- Per-device I/O queues with hierarchical scheduling
- Lock-free vs. locking trade-offs in hot paths
- Fairness and starvation prevention mechanisms
- Burst handling, credit reclamation, and idle time bonuses

## Minimal Pseudo-Code

```javascript
// Enqueue: add request to per-container queue, attempt to acquire device ticket
function enqueue(container_id, request) {
  container_queue = get_container_queue(container_id)
  container_queue.push(request)

  // Try to acquire dispatch token for this container
  if (container_credits[container_id] > 0) {
    device_queue = get_device_queue(request.device)
    if (device_queue.try_acquire_lock(container_id)) {
      dispatch(container_id, request.device)
    }
  }
}

// Dispatch: select next request using DRR, respecting weights and deficits
function dispatch(container_id, device) {
  device_queue = get_device_queue(device)

  // DRR: serve quantum, carry deficit to next round
  while (device_queue.has_pending(container_id)) {
    quantum = container_weight[container_id] * base_quantum
    deficit[container_id] += quantum

    while (deficit[container_id] > 0 && !container_queue.empty(container_id)) {
      request = container_queue.pop(container_id)
      service_time = estimate_cost(request)
      deficit[container_id] -= service_time
      issue_to_device(device, request)
    }

    // Move to next container if depleted
    container_id = device_queue.next_container(container_id)
  }
}

// Budget reclaimer: periodic task to reclaim unused credits
function reclaim_credits() {
  for each container in active_containers {
    if (idle_time[container] > idle_bonus_threshold) {
      container_credits[container] += idle_bonus
    }
    // Burn-in period for newly activated containers
    container_credits[container] = min(
      container_credits[container] + base_allocation,
      max_burst_limit
    )
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of process lifecycle, waitpid usage, and zombie avoidance in a practical multi-child scenario.

## Key Concepts
- waitpid semantics and looping
- SIGCHLD handling and reaping
- WNOHANG and non-blocking variants
- Zombie processes and edge cases (stopped/continued)

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
  // spawn N children ...
  int finished = 0; int status;
  while (finished < N) {
    pid_t pid = waitpid(-1, &status, 0);
    if (pid > 0) { finished++; /* process status */ }
    else if (pid == -1 && errno == ECHILD) break;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIGCHLD storms or large N?
- What are the risks of ignoring zombie cleanup in long-running daemons?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of high-performance I/O scheduling, lock-free data structures, and fairness under mixed workloads, mirroring real kernel-path concerns in storage stacks.

## Key Concepts
- I/O scheduling and per-core queues
- Lock-free enqueue/dequeue via CAS
- Request merging for sequential bursts
- Fairness and starvation mitigation via deadlines
- Realistic testing with mixed workloads and tail latency focus

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (conceptual)
typedef struct io_req { uint64_t lba; uint32_t len; int id; struct io_req *next; } io_req;
typedef struct { io_req *head, *tail; } ring_t;
static inline void io_enqueue(ring_t *q, io_req *r) { r->next = NULL; /* CAS-based tail append */ }
static inline io_req* io_dequeue(ring_t *q) { /* pop head */ return NULL; }
static inline int can_merge(io_req *a, io_req *b) { return a->lba + a->len == b->lba; }
static inline io_req* merge_adjacent(io_req *a, io_req *b) {
  if (!can_merge(a,b)) return NULL; a->len += b->len; return a;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical I/O scheduling fairness in a constrained, beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- I/O scheduling fairness and starvation avoidance
- Per-process credit system and aging
- Fixed-size request queue management
- Enqueue/dequeue logic and safe selection

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct IOReq {
  int pid;
  uint64_t lba;
  int size;
  int read; // 1 = read, 0 = write
  int credit; // dynamic, per-process
  int age;    // waiting cycles
  int arrival;
} IOReq;

int pick_next(IOReq q[], int n) {
  int best = -1;
  int best_prio = -1;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (q[i].arrival) {
      int prio = q[i].credit + (q[i].age > AGE_THRESHOLD ? 1 : 0);
      if (prio > best_prio) {
        best_prio = prio;
        best = i;
      }
    }
  }
  return best;
}
```

