## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assess understanding of signal semantics in multi-threaded processes and safe cleanup patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Signals and thread-level delivery
- Async-signal-safety and mutex interactions
- Self-pipe trick and deferred cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <signal.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int pipefd[2];

void sigint(int s){
  char c = 1;
  write(pipefd[1], &c, 1);
}

int main(){
  pipe(pipefd);
  struct sigaction sa;
  sa.sa_handler = sigint;
  sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
  sa.sa_flags = 0;
  sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL);

  char buf;
  while (read(pipefd[0], &buf, 1) > 0) {
    // termination requested
  }
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What happens if multiple signals arrive rapidly?
- How would you implement a robust termination sequence in a real program?

