## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of page fault handling, page cache coordination, IO paths, and race condition handling under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Page fault handling workflow: detection, frame allocation, IO vs zero-fill
- Per-page locking and CAS-based inflight signaling to serialize IO
- PTE updates, TLB invalidation, and waking waiters
- Throughput considerations under concurrent faults

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of per-page fault handling (pseudo-C)
typedef struct { int inflight; void* frame; int swapped; } Page;
void handle_fault(Page* p) {
  if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&p->inflight, 0, 1) == 0) {
    p->frame = allocate_frame();
    if (p->swapped) read_from_storage(p->frame);
    else zero_fill(p->frame);
    update_pte(p, p->frame);
    invalidate_tlb();
    wake_waiters(p);
    p->inflight = 0;
  } else {
    wait_for_completion(p);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness with multiple concurrent faulting threads?
- What are potential race conditions besides inflight handling, and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of race conditions, safe resource reuse, and lightweight OS primitives in a beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts

- Bitmaps for ID allocation
- Generation counters to distinguish reuse
- Simple locking strategy to avoid races
- Handling exhaustion and graceful error paths

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  uint16_t id;      // 0..65535
  uint16_t gen;     // generation counter
  bool in_use;
} pid_slot;

// Global state (simplified)
pid_slot table[65536];
pthread_mutex_t pid_lock;

int alloc_pid(void) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&pid_lock);
  for (uint32_t i = 1; i < 65536; i++) {
    if (!table[i].in_use) {
      table[i].in_use = true;
      table[i].gen++;
      int pid = (table[i].gen << 16) | i;
      pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
      return pid;
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
  return -1; // exhausted
}

void release_pid(uint32_t pid) {
  uint16_t idx = pid & 0xFFFF;
  pthread_mutex_lock(&pid_lock);
  table[idx].in_use = false;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement a non-blocking alloc_pid() without a global lock?
- How to handle PID namespace isolation across containers?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the candidate's understanding of file descriptor semantics, shared underlying objects, and race-free updates to per-process tables. It also assesses error handling and edge case coverage in a kernel interface.

## Key Concepts

- Per-process file descriptor table management
- Reference counting for shared file objects
- Atomic close-on-update semantics
- Robust input validation and error reporting

## Code Example

```c
int dup2(int oldfd, int newfd) {
    if (oldfd < 0 || oldfd >= 8 || newfd < 0 || newfd >= 8) {
        return -1;
    }
    if (oldfd == newfd) {
        return newfd;
    }

    lock_fd_table();
    if (newfd is open) {
        decrement_ref_count(newfd);
        clear_fd_entry(newfd);
    }
    copy_fd_entry(oldfd, newfd);
    increment_ref_count(newfd);
    unlock_fd_table();
    return newfd;
}
```

## Testing Approach

Test cases should cover: valid fd pairs with various reference counts, the oldfd == newfd case, boundary conditions (0 and 7), invalid oldfd, and newfd out of range. Also test dup2(-1, 5) returning -1, dup2(5, -1) returning -1, and scenarios where newfd is already open versus closed.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for dynamic participant counts?
- How would you test for spurious wakeups and ensure progress under contention?

